-- statement:
--   The theorem states a five-part conclusion about SourceGroup. The seed graph has as vertices the cells of a finite set: seven vertices (five of the form none or some(b,i) with b a Boolean and i in Fin 2, plus two indexed by a Boolean), 51 edges (six circle edges indexed by a Boolean and Fin 3, plus 45 sector edges), and 45 sectors; two cells are adjacent when one is a proper face of the other, where a vertex is a face of an edge or sector if it lies among that edge's endpoints or the sector's vertices, and an edge is a face of a sector if it is among the sector's three edges. The sectors come from two Boolean-indexed signed words encoding the relators x²y⁻⁵ and x²(yx⁻¹)³, of lengths 7 and 8, so the two relations have boundary lengths 21 and 24. The Artin group of this graph is the group generated by its cells, with a commutator relation for each pair of adjacent cells; the height map sends every generator to 1 in ℤ, and SourceGroup is the kernel of this height map. The theorem asserts that SourceGroup is finitely generated and residually finite, that its cohomological dimension, meaning the projective dimension of the trivial integral module ℤ over the integral group ring, equals 2, that there is a classifying space of dimension at most 3, and that for every universe u there is no classifying space of dimension at most 2. Here a classifying space of dimension n for G is a Hausdorff, path-connected space with a CW complex structure having no cells above dimension n and with fundamental group isomorphic to G, admitting a surjective covering map from a contractible space; the cell count is not bounded. The three-dimensional case is asserted in universe 0 and the two-dimensional nonexistence in an arbitrary universe u. The result is stated as an admitted theorem.
